 1 big thing: The surveillance election 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The 2026 midterms — a month from today! — will be the most intensely observed, recorded and dissected election in American history, Axios' Brittany Gibson writes. 

 Why it matters: An army of partisan and nonpartisan observers will join a surge of Justice Department monitors, organizers looking for ICE, and citizen journalists broadcasting their search for illegal voting in real time. Nonpartisan observation can bolster confidence in elections, studies show . But officials worry that activists expecting to find wrongdoing could misread routine events.

 After the 2020 election, claims from observers and activist sleuths helped fuel challenges. Those claims led to numerous lawsuits by the Trump campaign. The cases were almost all dismissed or denied relief. 🔎 Zoom in: DOJ plans to train 1,000 election monitors to deploy across the country, one of the largest-ever efforts from the agency. It builds on a significant increase in observers in 2024.

 Senate Democrats will use office staff to monitor the polls. That's in addition to a new 50-state recruitment program by a coalition of Democratic state parties, aiming to enlist at least 10,000 election watchers. The Republican National Committee is building on its election watch network set up in 2020 to counter perceived voter fraud. GOP partisans have signed up in record numbers to be poll workers , and the party is hosting virtual and in-person training for observers and challengers. A network of anti-ICE activists is transitioning ICE-watch training into poll-watch training, responding in part to the Trump administration saying ICE could make arrests at the polls. When asked about deploying agents, Trump told TIME he hadn't thought of it. President Trump speaks to reporters on the South Lawn yesterday before boarding Marine One on his way to a rally in Grand Island, Neb. Photo: Eric Lee/Reuters 🔭 Zoom out: These efforts will coexist with a slew of nonpartisan observing efforts, from voter protection groups to international election observers.

 State party and campaign staffers regularly monitor polls for mundane issues like accurate opening and closing times. White House spokeswoman Lauren Bis said: "Despite extreme Democrats and the media claiming it never happens, the evidence of voter fraud continues to pile up. Anyone who illegally votes in our elections will be held accountable." 

 More than two dozen federal charges have been brought this year against people for voting when they're not eligible, according to the Justice Department . A review by the nonpartisan Center for Election Innovation &amp; Research says instances of illegal voting are "extremely small." Share this story . 

 
 
