 3. 🎥 The Axios Show: &quot;People really want me to die&quot; 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Axios' Madison Mills interviews Bryan Johnson in Los Angeles yesterday. Photo: Daniel Hollis Diamond for Axios LOS ANGELES — Bryan Johnson — the face of the longevity movement, who believes he may never die — announced recently that he had an incurable autoimmune disease. Critics cheered, he said in a new episode of " The Axios Show ," out later this week.

 "I mean, I think people really want me to die. Now I get it. It's funny. It's ironic. I push a lot of buttons that people don't like," he told Axios' Madison Mills at his L.A. home, which doubles as a lifespan lab. The founder of the "Don't Die" movement sees his diagnosis as rocket fuel for his mission to promote longevity, rather than a setback. 💡 Catch up quick: Johnson, 49, has autoimmune gastritis (AIG), a chronic condition in which the immune system attacks healthy cells in the stomach lining.

 Johnson said "there was joy all around the world" after he announced his diagnosis in July. He spends millions of dollars a year on extreme health interventions, documenting the experiments for an online following of 8 million. 🥊 Johnson views the diagnosis as just another test of his campaign against aging: "I like the challenge. I'm up for it. I think it's good timing. I'm going to get diagnosed with something at some point. Why not this?"

