 5. 🤖 Inside Trump&#x27;s new AI playbook 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photo: Alex Grimm/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 President Trump's new "Super Intelligence" task force faces a daunting mandate: reconcile fiercely divided Cabinet factions to forge a tech policy that keeps pace with the blistering AI race, Axios' Maria Curi writes. 

 Why it matters: The task force brings the administration's scattered AI work under one roof. But it makes it likely that any new rules will be forged in the crossfire of competing internal camps. In an interview with Axios, task force co-chair Scott Kupor rejected the claim that the administration is asleep at the wheel on AI policy, arguing that rigid legislation could become obsolete overnight.

 "The worst thing for us to do would be to assume a static set of knowledge that allows us to put a regulatory schema in place that then has unintended consequences," said Kupor, a Silicon Valley veteran who is director of the U.S. Office of Personnel Management. Between the lines: The group's makeup highlights the competing fiefdoms driving Trump's AI strategy.

 Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and Trump Chief of Staff Susie Wiles have worked behind the scenes to encourage some oversight. On the other end of the spectrum, David Sacks and the Pentagon's Emil Michael are wary of slowing model development. Jay Clayton, the director of national intelligence and the new AI czar , is expected to bring cohesion to the group's collective work. More from the interview . 

 
 
